The PCA-based analysis proceeds as follows:
(i) for each experiment (i.e., each batch), arrange the corresponding data in the variable-wise unfolded matrix D (Wold et al., 1987) - the full dataset matrix D thus contains all
N
s p
​observations across all experiments. Apply PCA to project the observations of each experiment onto the score space, using the loadings on the principal components as coordinates, and compute the centroid for each experiment to represent the entire batch as a single point in the score space (Fig. A.1a);
(ii) compute the Euclidian distances between the centroids of all datasets to determine their similarity (Fig. A.1b);
(iii) identify the closest pair of centroids, i.e., the pair characterized by the minimum Euclidian distance (Fig. A.1c);
(iv) among the pair identified in (iii), the dataset that is closer to all other datasets, i.e., its mean Euclidian distance with respect to the other centroids is the lowest, indicates it is less unique (Fig. A.1d). When used for validation, this dataset ensures that no relevant information is lost.
